    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d234">

                <pb facs="0284" n="278" sameAs="frus:frus1942v03/pg278"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">811.20 (D) Regulations/5738: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Acting Secretary of State
                        to the Chargé in Spain</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Beaulac</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-02-21T16:00:00-04:00">February 21,
                            1942—4 p.m.</date>
                    </dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>108. The Department has just received a telegraphic report from Haven<anchor
                        n="29" xml:id="d234-f1"/> stating that pursuant to orders from Madrid CEPSA
                    refuses to furnish information petroleum products on hand. In view of the
                    assurances we have been given such as reported in your 99, January 22, midnight
                    and your 108, January 24, 3 p.m. the Department is unable to proceed with its
                    program now being worked out until full cooperation is realized.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Welles</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d234-f1" n="29" xml:id="d234-f1-content">
                <p>Richard B. Haven, Consul at Tenerife.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
